---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import SectionHead from '../components/ui/SectionHead.astro';

const messageEnvelope = `AbjectMessage {
  header: {
    messageId:      string    // unique identifier
    correlationId:  string?   // links replies to requests
    sequenceNumber: number    // per-sender ordering
    timestamp:      number    // milliseconds since epoch
    type:           "request" | "reply" | "event" | "error"
  }
  routing: {
    from:   AbjectId         // sender's runtime identity
    to:     AbjectId         // recipient's runtime identity
    method: string?          // handler name ("ask")
  }
  payload: T                 // method-specific data
  protocol: {
    version:       string    // protocol version
    negotiationId: string?   // agreement context
  }
}`;

const manifestStructure = `AbjectManifest {
  name:                 string
  description:          string
  version:              string
  interface: {
    id:          InterfaceId
    name:        string
    description: string
    methods: [{
      name:        string
      description: string
      params:      [{ name, type, description }]
      returns:     { type, description }
    }]
    events:  [{ name, description, payload }]
  }
  requiredCapabilities: CapabilityRequest[]
  providedCapabilities: CapabilityId[]?
  tags:                 string[]
}`;

/*
 * A small print-palette highlighter for the two type sketches: comments in
 * meta ink, string literals in brass, type names in the info blue, and the
 * structure's own name in heading ink. Escapes everything it emits.
 */
const esc = (s: string) => s.replace(/&/g, '&amp;').replace(/</g, '&lt;').replace(/>/g, '&gt;');
const TOKEN = /("[^"]*")|\b(string|number|AbjectId|InterfaceId|CapabilityRequest|CapabilityId|AbjectMessage|AbjectManifest)\b/g;
function highlight(src: string): string {
  return src
    .split('\n')
    .map((line) => {
      const cut = line.indexOf('//');
      const code = cut >= 0 ? line.slice(0, cut) : line;
      const note = cut >= 0 ? line.slice(cut) : '';
      let out = '';
      let last = 0;
      for (const m of code.matchAll(TOKEN)) {
        const at = m.index ?? 0;
        out += esc(code.slice(last, at));
        const cls = m[1] ? 't-str' : m[2] === 'AbjectMessage' || m[2] === 'AbjectManifest' ? 't-head' : 't-type';
        out += `<span class="${cls}">${esc(m[0])}</span>`;
        last = at + m[0].length;
      }
      out += esc(code.slice(last));
      if (note) out += `<span class="t-note">${esc(note)}</span>`;
      return out;
    })
    .join('\n');
}
---

<Layout title="The Ask Protocol - Technical Specification" description="The Ask Protocol: every object has a mandatory message handler called ask. Send it a question in plain English and it answers. Objects describe themselves without documentation or schemas, and work together without hand-written glue.">
  <Header />
  <main class="spec">
    <div class="container">

      <!-- Hero -->
      <header class="hero">
        <div class="hero-text band">
          <p class="kicker"><span class="sigil" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Technical Specification v1.0</span></p>
          <h1 class="hero-title">The Ask Protocol</h1>
          <span class="hero-bar" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <p class="lede">
            The internet is an object-oriented system. It's a bunch of computers sending messages to each other.
            The web put a document abstraction on top, and we forgot what was underneath.
            The Ask Protocol goes back to that idea and adds one message handler. Every object has to answer
            <code>ask</code>. You send it a question in plain English and it tells you what it does and how
            to work with it.
          </p>
          <div class="callout callout-hero">
            <strong>This isn't a proposal.</strong> It's a running system. Objects ask each other questions,
            generate proxies, and fix their broken connections today. <a href="/#downloads">Download it</a> and try it.
            If you want the why instead of the how, read <a href="/theory">the theory</a>.
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Table of Contents -->
        <nav class="toc" aria-labelledby="toc-title">
          <h2 class="toc-label" id="toc-title">Contents</h2>
          <ol class="toc-list">
            <li><a href="#fundamentals">How It Works</a></li>
            <li><a href="#message-format">Message Format and Flow</a></li>
            <li><a href="#self-describing">Objects That Describe Themselves</a></li>
            <li><a href="#use-cases">Use Cases</a></li>
            <li><a href="#comparison">How It Compares</a></li>
            <li><a href="#thesis">The Internet Is Object-Oriented</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>
      </header>

      <!-- Section 1: Protocol Fundamentals -->
      <section id="fundamentals" class="sec">
        <SectionHead num="01" title="How It Works" />

        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Every object in the system has a mandatory message handler called <code>ask</code>. The caller
            sends a natural-language question and the abject answers in natural language. If no model is
            available, it answers with a plain description of its manifest.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here is the whole protocol:
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="primitive">
          <h3 class="primitive-label">The Primitive</h3>
          <p class="primitive-sig"><code><span class="t-fn">ask</span>(question: <span class="t-type">string</span>) -&gt; <span class="t-type">string</span></code></p>
          <div class="primitive-notes">
            <p>
                The caller sends a question in plain English. The abject consults an LLM, giving it the
                question, its manifest (name, description, interfaces, capabilities), and even its own
                code, and answers the question the caller asked.
              </p>
            <p class="primitive-punch">
                That's the Ask Protocol. It's that simple.
              </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">

          <div class="callout">
            <strong>If you know Smalltalk or Erlang, you know most of this.</strong> The whole system is
            built as a bunch of objects communicating with each other, Smalltalk or Erlang style, where
            each object has this <code>ask</code> handler. Each abject has an address, a mailbox, its own
            private state, and handlers that process one message at a time. Ordinary messages work the way
            they do in Erlang or Akka: typed payloads, plain code, no LLM anywhere in the path. The only new
            rule is that <em>every object must implement <code>ask</code></em>, and ask is the one handler
            that may consult an LLM.
          </div>

          <p>
            Because every object implements <code>ask</code>, any object can ask any other object how it
            works. You don't need shared documentation, a schema registry, or a meeting to agree on
            formats. The object that has the behavior is the one that explains it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here's a concrete example. Imagine you have an object that manages a calendar and another
            that manages email. The email object receives a dinner invitation for Friday at 7pm. It
            doesn't have a hardcoded integration with the calendar object. Instead, it sends an ask
            message: <em>"I have a dinner invitation for Friday at 7pm, can you help me with this?"</em>
            The calendar object responds describing how it handles scheduling. The LLM generates the glue
            code on the fly, and the event gets created. No API documentation, no SDK, no integration
            sprint. Two objects that have never seen each other before work it out in plain language,
            just like cells reading chemical signals on each other's surfaces.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: Message Format & Flow -->
      <section id="message-format" class="sec">
        <SectionHead num="02" title="Message Format and Flow" />

        <div class="prose">
          <h3 class="sub">Message Envelope</h3>
          <p>
            Every message in the system uses the same envelope. Ask isn't special. It travels in the same
            <code>AbjectMessage</code> as everything else.
          </p>

          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption class="code-cap" aria-hidden="true">AbjectMessage</figcaption>
            <pre><code set:html={highlight(messageEnvelope)} /></pre>
          </figure>

          <p>
            For ask, the request payload is <code>{'{ question: string }'}</code> and the reply is a
            plain string with the object's answer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="sub">Flow</h3>
        </div>

        <figure class="fig-hero">
          <div class="fig-scroll">
            <svg class="flow-svg" viewBox="0 0 760 228" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-labelledby="flow-title">
              <title id="flow-title">Ask flow: the caller sends a question to the target, the target asks the LLM with its own self-knowledge as context, and returns the answer to the caller. Without an LLM the target answers with its manifest description.</title>
              <defs>
                <marker id="ap-live" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto">
                  <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="#5be5a0"/>
                </marker>
                <marker id="ap-aux" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto">
                  <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="#8f8a7b"/>
                </marker>
              </defs>

              <!-- Caller: a small window -->
              <rect class="d-node" x="20" y="24" width="120" height="72"/>
              <rect class="d-band" x="20" y="24" width="120" height="18"/>
              <circle class="d-sigil" cx="31" cy="33" r="4.5"/>
              <ellipse class="d-pupil" cx="31" cy="33" rx="1.2" ry="2.5"/>
              <text class="d-label" x="80" y="76" text-anchor="middle">Caller</text>

              <!-- Target: the key element, in the red band -->
              <rect class="d-node d-node-key" x="320" y="24" width="120" height="72"/>
              <rect class="d-band-key" x="320" y="24" width="120" height="18"/>
              <circle class="d-sigil d-sigil-key" cx="331" cy="33" r="4.5"/>
              <ellipse class="d-pupil" cx="331" cy="33" rx="1.2" ry="2.5"/>
              <text class="d-label d-label-key" x="380" y="76" text-anchor="middle">Target</text>

              <!-- LLM: a service the target consults -->
              <rect class="d-node d-node-muted" x="620" y="24" width="120" height="72"/>
              <rect class="d-band" x="620" y="24" width="120" height="18"/>
              <circle class="d-sigil d-sigil-muted" cx="631" cy="33" r="4.5"/>
              <text class="d-label d-label-muted" x="680" y="76" text-anchor="middle">LLM</text>

              <!-- Step 1: Caller -> Target (ask request) -->
              <line class="d-flow" x1="144" y1="58" x2="314" y2="58" marker-end="url(#ap-live)"/>
              <text class="d-msg" x="229" y="50" text-anchor="middle">{"ask({ question })"}</text>

              <!-- Step 2: Target -> LLM (with context) -->
              <line class="d-aux" x1="444" y1="58" x2="614" y2="58" marker-end="url(#ap-aux)"/>
              <text class="d-msg d-msg-dim" x="529" y="50" text-anchor="middle">self-knowledge</text>

              <!-- Step 3: LLM -> Target (answer) -->
              <line class="d-aux" x1="616" y1="82" x2="446" y2="82" marker-end="url(#ap-aux)"/>
              <text class="d-msg d-msg-dim" x="531" y="102" text-anchor="middle">LLM response</text>

              <!-- Step 4: Target -> Caller (reply) -->
              <line class="d-flow" x1="316" y1="82" x2="146" y2="82" marker-end="url(#ap-live)"/>
              <text class="d-msg" x="231" y="102" text-anchor="middle">answer (string)</text>

              <!-- Step labels -->
              <text class="d-step" x="80" y="132" text-anchor="middle"><tspan class="d-step-n">1.</tspan> Send question</text>
              <text class="d-step d-step-key" x="380" y="132" text-anchor="middle"><tspan class="d-step-n">2.</tspan> Assemble context</text>
              <text class="d-step d-step-muted" x="680" y="132" text-anchor="middle"><tspan class="d-step-n">3.</tspan> Generate answer</text>

              <!-- Fallback note -->
              <rect class="d-note" x="170" y="160" width="420" height="52"/>
              <text class="d-note-text" x="380" y="182" text-anchor="middle">Fallback: if no LLM is available, the target returns</text>
              <text class="d-note-text" x="380" y="199" text-anchor="middle">its manifest description as a plain-text answer.</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
        </figure>

                <ol class="stepper flow-steps">
          <li><span class="step-n" aria-hidden="true">01</span><p>The <strong>caller</strong> sends an <code>ask</code> request to the target with a natural-language question.</p></li>
          <li class="key"><span class="step-n" aria-hidden="true">02</span><p>The <strong>target</strong> finds the LLM service and gathers what it knows about itself.</p></li>
          <li><span class="step-n" aria-hidden="true">03</span><p>The <strong>LLM</strong> answers the question using that context.</p></li>
          <li><span class="step-n" aria-hidden="true">04</span><p>The <strong>target</strong> returns the answer to the caller.</p></li>
          <li><span class="step-n" aria-hidden="true">05</span><p>If no LLM is available, the target falls back to a plain manifest description.</p></li>
        </ol>

        <div class="prose">

          <p>
            The target speaks for itself. There is no outside schema or documentation service to check.
            The answer comes from the object that does the work.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: Self-Describing Objects -->
      <section id="self-describing" class="sec">
        <SectionHead num="03" title="Objects That Describe Themselves" />

        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Every abject carries a manifest that says what it is, what it can do, and which messages it
            handles. The Ask Protocol builds on it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="sub">The Manifest</h3>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption class="code-cap" aria-hidden="true">AbjectManifest</figcaption>
            <pre><code set:html={highlight(manifestStructure)} /></pre>
          </figure>

          <p>
            The manifest lists what the object is, the methods it handles, the events it sends, and the
            capabilities it needs and provides. The function <code>formatManifestAsDescription()</code>
            turns it into plain English, and that becomes the LLM's context when the object answers a
            question.
          </p>

          <h3 class="sub">Why Objects Can Answer</h3>

          <p>
            <code>ask</code> isn't a description field. It's a handler, code that runs every time someone
            asks the object something. That's why it does more than static documentation, and why an
            outside LLM reading a fixed description can't do the same job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="ledger answers">
          <div><span class="ix" aria-hidden="true">01</span><h4 class="t">Encapsulation, not a data dump</h4><p class="n">Like any object, an abject shows its interface and hides its internals. It answers <em>as</em> itself. It doesn't hand you a snapshot of everything inside.</p></div>
          <div><span class="ix" aria-hidden="true">02</span><h4 class="t">The answer depends on who asks</h4><p class="n">The <code>ask</code> handler receives the caller. A scheduler and a human can get different answers to the same question. A fixed description can't change with who is asking.</p></div>
          <div><span class="ix" aria-hidden="true">03</span><h4 class="t">Answering is code, not a lookup</h4><p class="n">The handler is code. It can check live state, negotiate, refuse, or even <em>ask the caller a question back</em>. It can be a two-way conversation. A description only goes one way.</p></div>
          <div><span class="ix" aria-hidden="true">04</span><h4 class="t">Always current</h4><p class="n">The answer is worked out when you ask, from the object's current manifest and code. Nobody writes it once and forgets to update it. When the object changes, its answers change.</p></div>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <h3 class="sub">The Introspect Interface</h3>
          <p>
            Every abject implements the <code>abjects:introspect</code> interface, with two methods:
          </p>
          <ul class="marks">
            <li><code>describe</code> returns the manifest and a formatted English description.</li>
            <li><code>ask</code> takes a natural-language question and answers it with an LLM, using what the object knows about itself as context.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            These aren't optional. Every object in the system has them, and I do mean every object: the UI,
            networking, the peer-to-peer layer, the agents, the chat. Any object can ask any other object
            about itself without setting anything up first.
          </p>

          <div class="callout">
            <strong>The point:</strong> objects can describe themselves without anyone having to write
            documentation, schemas, or anything else. A description someone wrote goes stale.
            <code>ask</code> runs every time, knows who is asking, and can ask back.
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4: Use Cases -->
      <section id="use-cases" class="sec">
        <SectionHead num="04" title="Use Cases" />

        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Ask is one small message. Here is what the system builds on top of it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mosaic uses">
          <article class="use u-oc solid-red">
            <h3 class="use-title"><span class="use-obj">ObjectCreator:</span> Code Generation</h3>
            <div class="use-body">
              <p>
                When you describe a new object in plain English, the ObjectCreator picks the objects it
                will depend on and writes questions for each one. It sends those questions with ask and
                uses the answers as context when it writes the new object's code.
              </p>
              <p class="use-moral">
                Objects teach new objects how to work with them.
              </p>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article class="use u-pg outline">
            <h3 class="use-title"><span class="use-obj">ProxyGenerator:</span> Protocol Translation</h3>
            <div class="use-body">
              <p>
                When two objects need to talk but speak different message formats, the ProxyGenerator
                asks both sides: "How should a proxy use your methods?" It gives both answers to the
                LLM and generates a translator proxy between them.
              </p>
              <p class="use-moral">
                The proxy isn't a hand-written shim. It's a real abject, generated from what the two
                objects said about themselves.
              </p>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article class="use u-sh paper">
            <h3 class="use-title"><span class="use-obj">Self-Healing:</span> Error Recovery</h3>
            <div class="use-body">
              <p>
                When two objects stop understanding each other, the system asks both of them how they
                should be used, collects the recent errors, and regenerates the proxy with all of that
                as context. The new proxy is built around the exact errors that broke the old one.
              </p>
              <p class="use-moral">
                Learning happens when things go wrong. Each new proxy knows what broke the last one.
              </p>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article class="use u-ch solid-brass">
            <h3 class="use-title"><span class="use-obj">Chat:</span> Human Interaction</h3>
            <div class="use-body">
              <p>
                You can ask any abject about itself, in plain English. It answers from its own manifest
                and code.
              </p>
              <p class="use-moral">
                There is no separate user manual. The object is the manual.
              </p>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article class="use u-tr">
            <h3 class="use-title"><span class="use-obj">Task Routing:</span> Approach-Based Dispatch</h3>
            <div class="use-body">
              <p>
                When a goal is planned, the planner asks the team of agents: "Can you do this? How?"
                Each agent looks at its own manifest and capabilities and answers with a concrete
                approach, or PASS. The planner gives each task to the agent whose approach fits,
                records it in the shared TupleSpace, and reads the results before planning the next
                round.
              </p>
              <p class="use-moral">
                Nobody matches task types to agent types. The agents explain what they would do, and
                the planner picks.
              </p>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article class="use u-ki outline">
            <h3 class="use-title"><span class="use-obj">KnowledgeBase:</span> Shared Memory</h3>
            <div class="use-body">
              <p>
                Abjects save what they learn (facts, lessons, insights, references, and patterns) in the
                workspace's KnowledgeBase, and other abjects search it with full-text queries. When you
                share a workspace, its knowledge copies to every peer in it through the SharedState
                CRDT. A private workspace's knowledge never leaves your machine.
              </p>
              <p class="use-moral">
                The knowledge is captured. What one abject learns, every abject in that workspace can
                use, on every peer that shares it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5: Comparison -->
      <section id="comparison" class="sec">
        <SectionHead num="05" title="How It Compares" />

        <div class="prose">
          <h3 class="sub">Actor Systems</h3>
          <p>
            The closest relative isn't a web protocol at all. It's Smalltalk and Erlang. Abjects <em>are</em>
            actors in the classic Hewitt and Erlang sense. Each one has an address, a mailbox, and private
            state, handles one message at a time, and gets restarted by a supervisor when it crashes. If
            you have written Erlang/OTP or Akka, the runtime will feel familiar, down to the bounded
            mailboxes.
          </p>
          <p>
            The hard part of these systems was always the moment two actors meet that don't share the
            same messages. They have interfaces that need to work together, and when one interface
            changes, anything talking to it breaks. In Erlang, people solve that by hand: developers read
            each other's docs, agree on message shapes, and ship matching code. The Ask Protocol moves that
            step into the runtime. Every actor answers <code>ask</code>, so you can ask a stranger how it
            works, and the glue code people used to write by hand is generated from its answers. The LLM
            only comes in there, when two objects first meet. After that it's ordinary message passing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="sub">Web Protocols</h3>
          <p>
            REST, GraphQL, and gRPC are how services talk to each other on the web today. All three need a
            person to read the documentation, write the integration code, and fix it when something changes.
          </p>
          <p>
            This problem isn't new. CORBA in the 1990s, SOAP and WSDL in the 2000s, and later protocols
            like UPnP all tried to make services self-describing so they could discover and talk to each
            other. They all collapsed under the weight of rigid schemas, verbose XML contracts, and version
            incompatibilities. Natural language succeeds where schemas failed because it is flexible. An
            LLM can handle ambiguity, paraphrase, and partial information in a way that WSDL negotiation
            never could.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="cmp-wrap">
          <table class="cmp">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <td class="cmp-corner"></td>
                <th scope="col">REST</th>
                <th scope="col">GraphQL</th>
                <th scope="col">gRPC</th>
                <th scope="col" class="ask">Ask Protocol</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Self-Description</th>
                <td data-col="REST">OpenAPI specs. Static. External to the service.</td>
                <td data-col="GraphQL">Schema introspection. Structural, not behavioral.</td>
                <td data-col="gRPC">Proto reflection. Method listing, not explanation.</td>
                <td data-col="Ask Protocol" class="ask">The object answers from its own self-knowledge. Dynamic.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Runtime Adaptation</th>
                <td data-col="REST">None. Versioned endpoints.</td>
                <td data-col="GraphQL">None. Schema evolution.</td>
                <td data-col="gRPC">None. Proto versioning.</td>
                <td data-col="Ask Protocol" class="ask">Proxy regeneration with error context.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Natural Language</th>
                <td data-col="REST">No.</td>
                <td data-col="GraphQL">No.</td>
                <td data-col="gRPC">No.</td>
                <td data-col="Ask Protocol" class="ask">Yes. Objects answer in English.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Composition</th>
                <td data-col="REST">Human reads docs, writes client.</td>
                <td data-col="GraphQL">Human writes queries against schema.</td>
                <td data-col="gRPC">Human writes client from proto.</td>
                <td data-col="Ask Protocol" class="ask">Objects interview each other.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Error Recovery</th>
                <td data-col="REST">Retry. Circuit breaker.</td>
                <td data-col="GraphQL">Partial results.</td>
                <td data-col="gRPC">Retry policy.</td>
                <td data-col="Ask Protocol" class="ask">Regenerate translator with error history.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <h3 class="sub">LLM and Agent Protocols</h3>
          <p>
            MCP, A2A, and OpenAI's function calling are the new protocols for hooking LLMs up to tools,
            and agents up to other agents. Each one solves a piece of the problem. The Ask Protocol sits
            somewhere else. It isn't a tool-calling convention or an agent orchestration layer. It's a
            message every object answers about itself.
          </p>
          <p>
            In TCP, DNS, MCP, and A2A, negotiating capabilities is a step on the way to the real work,
            which happens in a layer above. The Ask Protocol has no layer above it. In a peer mesh there is
            no top. Objects asking each other questions, generating proxies, and fixing misunderstandings
            <em>is</em> the work.
          </p>

          <div class="callout">
            <strong>The difference:</strong> MCP is a band-aid. A2A is a band-aid. Everyone is building
            plumbing between things that shouldn't need plumbing. Plumbing tells you <em>how</em> to call
            something. Ask tells you <em>what</em> it is and <em>why</em> you would call it, in the object's
            own words, backed by its own code.
          </div>

          <p>
            The table below isn't a scorecard. Ask sits at a different layer than these, so read it as a
            map of where each one lives.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="cmp-wrap">
          <table class="cmp">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <td class="cmp-corner"></td>
                <th scope="col">MCP</th>
                <th scope="col">A2A</th>
                <th scope="col">OpenAI Function Calling</th>
                <th scope="col" class="ask">Ask Protocol</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Purpose</th>
                <td data-col="MCP">Connect LLM apps to external tools and data sources.</td>
                <td data-col="A2A">Agent-to-agent task delegation and coordination.</td>
                <td data-col="OpenAI Function Calling">Let models invoke developer-defined functions.</td>
                <td data-col="Ask Protocol" class="ask">Let objects explain themselves and teach each other.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Self-Description</th>
                <td data-col="MCP">Servers declare tools, resources, prompts at connect time. Static JSON schemas.</td>
                <td data-col="A2A">Agent Cards: signed JSON with identity, skills, security schemes.</td>
                <td data-col="OpenAI Function Calling">Developer provides JSON schemas with descriptions per function.</td>
                <td data-col="Ask Protocol" class="ask">The object answers from its own self-knowledge. As the object changes, the answers change.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Who Describes?</th>
                <td data-col="MCP">The server developer writes tool descriptions.</td>
                <td data-col="A2A">The agent developer writes the Agent Card.</td>
                <td data-col="OpenAI Function Calling">The app developer defines function schemas.</td>
                <td data-col="Ask Protocol" class="ask">The object describes itself. No developer in the loop at query time.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Object-to-Object</th>
                <td data-col="MCP">No. Tools serve the LLM, not each other.</td>
                <td data-col="A2A">Yes. Agents delegate tasks to other agents.</td>
                <td data-col="OpenAI Function Calling">No. Functions serve the model.</td>
                <td data-col="Ask Protocol" class="ask">Yes. Any object can ask any other object. No central coordinator.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            They can still work together. MCP could carry an <code>ask</code> call, and A2A could route it
            between peers. But neither one gives you objects that describe themselves. Ask is the content,
            and they are the envelope. It also pays off only when the <em>whole system</em> works this way,
            where every participant can be asked <em>and</em> can ask back. Bolted onto a single server, it's
            just another description field.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6: Thesis -->
      <section id="thesis" class="sec">
        <SectionHead num="06" title="The Internet Is Object-Oriented" />

        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            The internet is a bunch of computers sending messages to each other. TCP/IP, DNS, and BGP are
            protocols between machines that identify themselves, negotiate what they can do, and exchange
            data. There are an estimated 30 billion connected devices, and since the internet started, all
            the atoms in the system have been replaced without it ever shutting down. That's the power of
            great architecture.
          </p>

          <p>
            Biology got there first. Cells are like little computers and talk to each other with chemical
            messages. Crucially, cells don't communicate through a central specification document. A white
            blood cell encountering a pathogen doesn't look up an API. It reads chemical signals on the cell
            surface and responds. The Ask Protocol gives objects on a network the same ability.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask doesn't replace TCP or DNS. It doesn't move any bytes. It works one level up, at the level
            of meaning. TCP answers "how do I reach you?" Ask answers "what are you, and why would I call
            you?"
          </p>

          <p>
            The web is an application on top of the internet. HTTP put a document metaphor on it, with
            URLs as resource locators and verbs as CRUD operations. GraphQL added a query metaphor, and gRPC
            a procedure metaphor. All three hide what's underneath: a network of objects sending messages.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Ask Protocol doesn't add object orientation to the internet. It was always there. What ask
            adds is the part that was missing: objects that describe themselves, teach strangers how to use
            them, and adapt when communication breaks down.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="props">
          <div class="prop">
            <h4>Identity</h4>
            <p>Every peer has a PeerId (the SHA-256 hash of its public signing key) and every object a TypeId (a scoped, durable name under that peer). Nobody assigns these. They come from cryptography, not a registry.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="prop">
            <h4>Behavior</h4>
            <p>Every object has handlers, named methods that process messages. The manifest declares them and code implements them. You can inspect, query, and change them while the system runs.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="prop">
            <h4>Self-Knowledge</h4>
            <p>Every object carries its own manifest and answers questions about itself through ask. There is no outside documentation to look up.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="prop">
            <h4>Negotiation</h4>
            <p>When two objects with incompatible interfaces meet, the system generates a translator proxy from what each one says about itself. Two strangers can work together without agreeing on anything first.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Add a peer-to-peer layer (WebRTC, addressed by identity) and objects on different machines can
            find each other, ask what the other does, and work together. Self-aware objects finding each other
            and talking to each other without you. Now THAT is scary.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is no central schema registry, no shared proto files, and no API documentation site.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="poster-quote thesis-conclusion">
          All you have to do is ask.
        </p>
      </section>

